      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The aim of this study was to validate noninvasive models, retrieved from the literature, estimating body fat in white women. The cohort used for the validation consisted of 277 postmenopausal women, and the reference method was dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA). Five models were retrieved containing anthropometric measurements such as bicep and tricep skinfolds, waist circumference (WC), height, and body weight. Models including only BMI were found to be less biased and more valid than others including skinfolds and circumferences. The model by Visser et al., estimating body density (BD = 0.0226 × sex - 0.0022 × BMI + 1.0605) with the subsequent use of Brozek's (and not Siri's) equation to estimate body fat percentage (%BF), was found to be more valid than the other models for this cohort. In conclusion, it seems that Visser's et al. model, including only BMI, with Brozek's equation, is a fast, noninvasive, and valid method to assess body composition in white postmenopausal women in clinical practice and research."},
